Elastic constitutive laws and the assumptions they carry (@mech-elastic-law: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @mechanical-engineering, #source "../sources/Mechanics-of-Materials-Roylance.lingua#r_7K2RYA9504FGFGJTVWHMWXD5WY", #source "../sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W", #mechanical-engineering "Thermal-strain-constraints-and-residual-stress.lingua#r_0755DVWWAGZWYN0R75V7EDBR61", #materials-engineering "../../materials-engineering/mechanical/Elastic-energy-anisotropy-and-the-measured-modulus.lingua#r_5478VF901Q60ZK34Z449WT9BPE") { r_7PD9HSJTERFS77Q3TRSX6CAVP1
**Read:** [Modules in Mechanics of Materials — David Roylance](../sources/Mechanics-of-Materials-Roylance.lingua#r_7K2RYA9504FGFGJTVWHMWXD5WY), Constitutive Relations and Introduction to Composites; [Applied Mechanics of Solids — Allan F. Bower](../sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W), sections 3.1–3.5.

Equilibrium and geometry do not determine material response by themselves. A constitutive law connects suitable stress, strain, temperature and history variables. In isotropic small-strain linear elasticity, σ = 2Gε + λ tr(ε)1 for the mechanical strain, with G = E/(2(1+ν)) and λ = Eν/((1+ν)(1−2ν)). The same law can be written in compliance form; verify that the forms invert each other.

For a stable three-dimensional isotropic elastic solid in this model, E > 0 and −1 < ν < 0.5. Approaching ν = 0.5 represents near incompressibility and requires attention to numerical formulation. Plane stress sets specified out-of-plane stresses to zero; plane strain sets specified out-of-plane strains to zero. They give different reduced stiffnesses.

With E = 200 GPa, ν = 0.3 and uniaxial stress 100 MPa, axial strain is 0.0005 and each lateral strain is −0.00015. A hydrostatic load probes the bulk modulus rather than this uniaxial compliance. For an oriented composite, stiffness depends on material axes and more independent constants.

**Work:** Derive the uniaxial, shear and hydrostatic responses from the tensor law and check positive stored energy. Compare plane stress and plane strain under the same in-plane deformation. For a rubber or large-deformation component, state why a hyperelastic energy law and finite strain measures may be needed instead of extending Hooke's law indefinitely.

